8:### 0851 — An NE lifecycle read from the SDK header alone was wrong in the direction that mattered (TASK-260728-7ii1xz, r2)
9:- ROOT CAUSE: r1 concluded "the app cannot reach a stopped provider" from the macOS 26.5 SDK header for `handleAppMessage:` — *"called by the framework when the container app sends a message to the provider"*. That sentence names **who sends**, not a liveness precondition, and the header for the sending side says nothing about launch at all. Apple's published API reference does: `sendProviderMessage(_:responseHandler:)` — *"If the extension is not running, **it should be launched to handle the message**."* One inference from a header that was silent, and revocation was declared permanently asynchronous, `379cpk` AC4 was declared unsatisfiable, and a 60-second startup budget was spent waiting for a seed. **Generalisable: for Network Extension lifecycle questions the shipped header and the published reference are not interchangeable — the header documents the signature, the reference documents the behaviour.** Check both before designing around an absence.
12:- FINDING: `kSecUseKeychain` is **add-only**. `SecItem.h` defines it as "a value of type `SecKeychainRef` to which **`SecItemAdd`** will add the provided item(s)"; scoping a *search* takes `kSecMatchSearchList`. r1 specified `kSecUseKeychain` for the query. Verified end to end against a throwaway keychain — add/read/delete all `OSStatus=0`, and a **negative control** with the search list pointed at the user default returns `-25300 errSecItemNotFound`, proving the list actually scopes rather than being silently ignored. This also gives `379cpk`/`1o9wjz` a real unit-test seam needing neither root nor the System keychain.
13:- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
15:- DECISION: A settled decision row must have its **pending scaffolding deleted with it**. The ypo7yo matrix row still carried `resolutionOwner: TASK-260728-7ii1xz` and an `amendmentRule` reading "if the transport gives the provider direct keychain access, amend to `required` with the group literal". The selected transport makes that antecedent **true** while the file-based keychain makes the consequent **false** — so leaving it in place was a live instruction to re-grant the row on precisely the reasoning the decision refutes. The amendment packet now covers all five keys on the row field by field. Worth a validator gate: a `prohibited` row that still names a `resolutionOwner` is a contradiction.
17:### 0822 — macOS credential transport decided: the root provider owns a System keychain item, seeded once over the NE app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz)
18:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
19:- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
23:- FINDING: One property macOS **cannot** offer that iOS does: the System keychain unlocks at boot from root-only `/private/var/db/SystemKey`, so the secret is **not** protected by the user's login password. ~~(2) deletion cannot be synchronous — the user-context app cannot delete a root-owned item while the provider is stopped, so revocation is a provider request reconciled by a start-time sweep.~~ **CORRECTED in r2 — see the 0851 entry.** Revocation *is* synchronous whenever the NE configuration is installed and enabled; the r1 claim rested on a misread SDK header.
24:- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
26:- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
49:- ANOMALY: Every Apple example of an app group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping products inspected here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — and all predate Feb 2025. No source found confirms an iOS-style ID works as a Mach service prefix. Not blocking: the spec's host↔provider channel is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references (`.spec/architecture.md:111`), not XPC. Recorded as **OC-4** owned by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, with the amendment path being a separate *unregistered* macOS-style ID for that one purpose. This supersedes the 0657 claim below that the macOS group is required "as the XPC Mach-service namespace" — that purpose is now conditional, not established.
50:- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.
61:- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.

